BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Grim History of the Death Penalty in Canada: Author Talk with 
 Lorna Poplak
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260826T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260826T200000
UID:6a3b052896ee38a44a92a857@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Death by hanging was a grim realty within the Canadian crimina
 l justice system until its abolition in 1976 (now 50 years ago). But what 
 did capital punishment look like in Canada? Author Lorna Poplak will discu
 ss the history of the death penalty in Canada\, with a focus on topics suc
 h as the inconsistent application of the death sentence\, the treatment of
  women\, wrongful conviction\, the difficult journey toward abolition\, an
 d the last two hangings at Toronto's notorious Don Jail.\nLorna Poplak is 
 a writer\, editor\, and researcher\, with a fascination for the stories be
 hind the facts of Canadian history. Her books include\nDrop Dead\n\,\nThe 
 Don\n\, and\nOn the Lam\n. She lives in Toronto.\nQ&A to follow. Signed bo
 oks available for purchase.
LOCATION:Deer Park\, Upstairs in room 204
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a3b052896ee38a44a92a857
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
